            <ArticleTitle>Midwifery students attribute different quantitative meanings to hurt, ache and pain: a cross-sectional survey.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.wombi.2012.11.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1871-5192(12)00094-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Assessment of women's labor pain is seldom acknowledged in clinical practice or research. The words &quot;aching&quot; and &quot;hurting&quot; are frequently used by women to describe childbirth pain. The aim of this study was to determine the quantitative meanings midwifery students attribute to the terms &quot;hurt&quot;, &quot;ache&quot; and &quot;pain&quot;. Data was collected by self-administered questionnaire from students at seven Swedish midwifery programs. A total of 230 filled out and returned a completed questionnaire requesting them to rate, on a visual analog scale, the intensity of &quot;hurt&quot;, &quot;ache&quot; or &quot;pain&quot; in the back, as reported by a fictitious parturient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The midwifery students attributed, with substantial individual variation, different quantitative meanings to the studied pain descriptors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To be able to communicate about pain with a woman in labor, it is essential that the midwife be familiar with the value of different words and what they mean to her as this may affect her assessment when the woman describes her pain.</AbstractText>
